      "s65-c7-iapost",
      "Ongoing Duties for Advisers",
      "Once registered, firms and individuals must keep meeting rules to stay registered.\n\nState-registered investment advisers must:\n• Meet net capital rules at all times. Each state sets its own, but none can exceed the Investment Advisers Act of 1940.\n• File financial reports when required, and update them as soon as possible if facts change.\n• Keep records. Most client records and advertising must be kept for five years, the first two in an easy-to-reach place. The SEC also requires federal covered advisers to keep most records for five years. Founding papers, like partnership articles, corporate charters, minute books, and stock certificate books, must be kept for the life of the firm plus three years after it closes. A state cannot require more records than the adviser's home state does.\n• Allow audits. The Administrator can audit any adviser registered in the state when it is in the public interest and look at any useful records. It cannot audit a federal covered adviser unless the adviser's main office is in that state.\n• Post a surety bond, in cash, securities, or bond insurance, if required, when it has custody or discretion.\n• Renew and pay fees by December 31. Federal covered advisers also pay a yearly state filing fee.\n• Update Form ADV within 90 days after the end of the fiscal year. Material changes during the year must be filed promptly by federal covered advisers and within 30 days by state-registered advisers.\nStates can set different bonding, net capital, and recordkeeping rules, but none can exceed the adviser's home state.\n\nIARs must renew and pay fees by the end of each year. They have no net capital requirement.",
